Imports:
  - Types:
      - LazyLoader
      - NamedLoaderContext
    From: salt/loader

Annotations: |
  This cell is salt/grains — 13 files that compute static, per-minion facts about the host
  (OS, hardware, network interfaces, ...). core.py alone contributes dozens of individual grain
  functions and dominates this category; the other 12 files (disks.py, mdadm.py, lvm.py,
  package.py, ...) each contribute a handful more. Grains are collected into a `LazyLoader`
  (salt.loader.grain_funcs, tag "grains") and computed very early in minion startup — before
  __salt__/__proxy__ exist — so grain functions only ever see __opts__ and
  previously-computed __grains__ as `NamedLoaderContext` proxies (plus, on proxy minions, the
  proxy connection module). Results are deep-merged across every grains file and, when
  grains_cache is enabled, persisted to <cachedir>/grains.cache.p to avoid recomputing
  expensive hardware probes on every restart.

---

"__init__(opts: dict[str, object])":
  location: core.py
  annotations: |
    Optional module-level hook LazyLoader._load_module calls once, immediately after importing
    this file and before running any of its individual grain functions — the same convention
    execution modules use for __init__(opts), distinct from the per-function __virtual__ gate
    used in salt/modules.

"os_data() -> data:dict[str, object]":
  location: core.py
  annotations: |
    Compute the OS-identification family of grains in one pass: os, os_family, oscodename,
    osfullname, osrelease, osrelease_info, osmajorrelease, kernel, kernelrelease, and
    related keys, using whichever platform-detection strategy applies (/etc/os-release,
    platform module calls, Windows registry, etc.). The largest single grain-producing function
    in the codebase.

"path() -> data:dict[str, str]":
  location: core.py
  annotations: |
    Return the minion process's PATH environment variable as the `path` grain (and its systempath
    alias), unparsed.

